::ENG2_U05_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best fits school leaders as the audience?{
~A. The library should stay open because it is awesome.#Incorrect. Awesome is too informal for school leaders.
=B. Extended library hours would provide students with a supervised academic workspace after school.#Correct. The revision is formal, precise, and audience-focused.
~C. Kids need a cool spot.#Incorrect. Kids and cool spot are informal.
~D. I want the library open because I like it.#Incorrect. This is personal instead of audience-focused.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which phrase has the most precise formal tone?{
~A. good place#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. cool area#Incorrect. This is informal.
~C. nice thing#Incorrect. This is vague.
=D. supervised academic workspace#Correct. This phrase is specific and formal.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is correctly written?{
=A. Students need a quiet place where they can complete assignments.#Correct. The sentence is complete and follows standard English.
~B. Students needs a quiet place.#Incorrect. Students needs should be students need.
~C. Students cannot focus good.#Incorrect. Good should be well.
~D. Because students need a quiet place.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word is a derivation of revise?{
~A. visible#Incorrect. Visible is not derived from revise.
~B. visit#Incorrect. Visit is unrelated to revising writing.
=C. revision#Correct. Revision is derived from revise.
~D. vision#Incorrect. Vision is not the noun for changing a draft.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Feedback: "Your wording is too casual." Which revision best responds?{
=A. The proposal would support students who need a quiet place to study after school.#Correct. This revision replaces casual wording with formal, precise language.
~B. The proposal is super cool.#Incorrect. Super cool is still casual.
~C. People will like it a lot.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~D. It is a nice thing.#Incorrect. This does not improve precision or tone.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word has a formal positive connotation?{
~A. awesome#Incorrect. Awesome is positive but casual.
~B. neat#Incorrect. Neat is informal.
~C. okay#Incorrect. Okay is informal and weak.
=D. beneficial#Correct. Beneficial is formal and means producing a good result.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision fixes the error in "students cannot focus good"?{
~A. students cannot focus good#Incorrect. The error remains.
=B. students cannot focus well#Correct. Well correctly describes how students focus.
~C. students cannot focused good#Incorrect. The verb form and adverb are incorrect.
~D. students cannot good focus#Incorrect. The word order is not standard.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best explains the specific student need?{
~A. The library is useful.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~B. The library is a good thing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. Students who wait for late buses need a quiet supervised space to complete assignments.#Correct. This identifies the student group and the need.
~D. People need it.#Incorrect. People and it are unclear.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which pair belongs to the same word family?{
~A. quiet and loud#Incorrect. These are opposites, not a word family.
~B. school and student#Incorrect. These are topic-related, not derivations.
~C. helpful and hallway#Incorrect. These are unrelated.
=D. precise and precision#Correct. Precision is the noun form related to precise.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which explanation best supports a polish choice?{
=A. I changed "kids" to "students" because the audience is formal and school-based.#Correct. This explanation connects the word choice to the audience.
~B. I changed the word because it was longer.#Incorrect. Longer words are not automatically better.
~C. I added slang to sound friendly.#Incorrect. Slang weakens formal audience fit.
~D. I deleted the reason because the sentence was hard.#Incorrect. Removing reasoning weakens the writing.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which choice best replaces "good" in "a good place to work" for a formal audience?{
~A. cool#Incorrect. Cool is informal.
~B. nice#Incorrect. Nice is vague.
=C. productive#Correct. Productive is more precise and formal.
~D. pretty#Incorrect. Pretty does not fit the intended meaning.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence avoids a comma splice?{
~A. The library is quiet, students can study there.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
=B. The library is quiet, so students can study there.#Correct. The conjunction so correctly joins the ideas.
~C. The library is quiet students can study there.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~D. Because the library is quiet.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which is the best final polish checklist?{
=A. Check audience, precise wording, tone, sentence flow, and conventions.#Correct. This checklist matches audience-focused polish.
~B. Add difficult words and ignore the audience.#Incorrect. Difficult words do not guarantee clarity.
~C. Make every sentence short and delete evidence.#Incorrect. This does not support the message.
~D. Change the topic if feedback is difficult.#Incorrect. Revision improves the draft's assigned purpose.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence has the strongest formal style?{
~A. The library is awesome for everybody.#Incorrect. Awesome and everybody are too casual and broad.
~B. The library thing helps people.#Incorrect. Thing and people are vague.
~C. It is cool after school.#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
=D. Extended hours would benefit students who need a quiet study environment.#Correct. This wording is formal, precise, and audience-aware.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word completes this word family: revise, revision, _____?{
~A. river#Incorrect. River is unrelated.
~B. visual#Incorrect. Visual is unrelated to revising a draft.
=C. revisable#Correct. Revisable means able to be revised and belongs to the word family.
~D. visitor#Incorrect. Visitor is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence uses a pronoun clearly?{
~A. When students asked teachers about the library, they said it should stay open.#Incorrect. They is unclear.
=B. Students told teachers that the library should stay open after school.#Correct. The sentence clearly names who spoke and what should stay open.
~C. They said it should stay open after they talked to them.#Incorrect. The pronouns are unclear.
~D. Students told teachers that it should stay open.#Incorrect. It is still less clear than naming the library.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision keeps the meaning while improving polish?{
~A. Students do not need a place after school.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~B. The school should stop helping students.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
=C. Students need a supervised study space after school.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and improves precision.
~D. A library is a building with books.#Incorrect. This shifts away from the claim.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word choice is too informal for school leaders?{
=A. awesome#Correct. Awesome is casual and does not fit a formal proposal.
~B. academic#Incorrect. Academic is formal.
~C. supervised#Incorrect. Supervised is formal and precise.
~D. beneficial#Incorrect. Beneficial is formal.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is complete?{
~A. Because the hallways are loud.#Incorrect. This is a dependent clause fragment.
=B. Students wait in loud hallways after school.#Correct. This is a complete sentence.
~C. While students wait after school.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. A quiet place for students.#Incorrect. This is a phrase, not a complete sentence.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence best addresses audience needs?{
~A. You should just do this.#Incorrect. This is demanding and informal.
~B. I want it, so it should happen.#Incorrect. This is writer-centered.
~C. Everyone knows libraries are good.#Incorrect. This is vague and unsupported.
=D. School leaders should consider extended hours because students need a supervised place to study while waiting for transportation.#Correct. This is respectful, specific, and audience-aware.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which suffix changes precise into a noun meaning exactness?{
=A. -ion#Correct. Precision is the noun form connected to precise.
~B. -less#Incorrect. This suffix usually means without.
~C. -er#Incorrect. This suffix can indicate a person or comparison, not this noun.
~D. -ful#Incorrect. This suffix means full of.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which explanation correctly discusses connotation?{
~A. Beneficial and awesome have exactly the same tone.#Incorrect. Beneficial is formal, while awesome is casual.
~B. Awesome is always incorrect in every sentence.#Incorrect. Awesome can fit informal contexts.
=C. Beneficial sounds formal and appropriate for a proposal to school leaders.#Correct. This explains the word's connotation in context.
~D. Connotation only means spelling.#Incorrect. Connotation means feeling or association.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision corrects the subject-verb agreement error? "The students needs quiet space."{
~A. The students needs quiet space.#Incorrect. The verb still does not agree.
~B. The students needing quiet space.#Incorrect. This is not a complete sentence.
~C. The student need quiet spaces.#Incorrect. Student does not agree with need.
=D. The students need a quiet space.#Correct. Students agrees with need, and the sentence is complete.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision is most polished?{
~A. Kids need a thing after school.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
=B. Students who remain on campus after school need a quiet supervised space for academic work.#Correct. This is precise, formal, and audience-focused.
~C. It is good for them.#Incorrect. The pronouns and wording are vague.
~D. A place is nice for work.#Incorrect. This is vague and awkward.
}



::ENG2_U05_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] What should a writer do before submitting a polished draft?{
=A. Reread for audience fit, precise wording, sentence control, and conventions.#Correct. This final check matches the lesson's polish process.
~B. Replace every simple word with a harder word.#Incorrect. The goal is precision, not difficulty.
~C. Ignore feedback after making one change.#Incorrect. Useful feedback should guide revision.
~D. Remove all transitions from the paragraph.#Incorrect. Transitions can improve flow and clarity.
}
